# Example plaintext dotenv file, the state BEFORE encryption.
#
# Copy to <repo>/opentofu/secrets.sops.env, put real values in, then encrypt
# it in place from inside the repo so SOPS finds .sops.yaml:
#
#   cd <repo>
#   sops encrypt -i opentofu/secrets.sops.env
#
# Mind the gap. Between writing real values here and encrypting, a plaintext
# secret is sitting at a path .gitignore deliberately does not ignore. A
# `git add -A` in that window commits it in the clear.
#
# SOPS encrypts comments too, so delete this header before you encrypt unless
# you want it in the committed file as ciphertext.
#
# Keys stay readable after encryption, values do not. Only put things here
# that actually need encrypting. Everything else belongs in .envrc as a plain
# export, where it stays readable in review.
#
# No quotes. The dotenv store takes everything after the first = as the
# value, quote characters included. One value per line.
#
# After encrypting:
#   sops filestatus opentofu/secrets.sops.env     # {"encrypted":true}
#   grep -c map_recipient opentofu/secrets.sops.env
#   git check-ignore -q opentofu/secrets.sops.env; echo $?    # expect 1

PROXMOX_VE_API_TOKEN=root@pam!terraform=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
TF_STATE_PASSPHRASE=replace-me-with-something-long
